  "prompt_text": "This figure gives a conceptual overview of the water cycle from local land–atmosphere exchange to the larger river-basin scale, while highlighting where humans can alter it. Water moves through precipitation, evapotranspiration, plant transpiration, runoff, soil moisture, surface water, groundwater, and the cryosphere, with atmospheric transport linking land and air and runoff draining into rivers.\n\nThe left side emphasizes the local water balance. It shows how land properties such as topography, land cover, soils, and geology influence storage and flow, and how human activities modify these processes. Visible examples include urbanization, agriculture, irrigation, de- or afforestation, and human emissions, which appear connected to precipitation, evaporation, transpiration, and subsurface water.\n\nThe right side scales this up to a river basin. River flow integrates runoff and groundwater interactions and eventually connects to the ocean or inland sinks. Human interventions become more direct here, including reservoirs and dams, flood protection, water withdrawal for domestic and industrial use, irrigation, and diversion to other basins.\n\nOverall, the image shows that water movement depends on coupled atmospheric, terrestrial, and river-basin processes, and that multiple kinds of human influence can reshape both local hydrology and basin-wide water distribution.",
